\section{What \rex{} means by an explanation}

\subsection*{The base notion}

Let $\vec V$ be Boolean variables in correspondence with the pixels of an image
$x$, where $v_i = 1$ means pixel $p_i$ keeps its original value and $v_i = 0$
means it is replaced by a masking value; let $O$ record whether the masked image
still classifies as $N(x)$. A subset $\vec V_{\mathrm{exp}}$ is an
\emph{explanation} when

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
\item[\textsc{exim}1.] $(M, \vec u_0) \models [\vec V_{\mathrm{exp}} = 1](O = 1)$
--- setting those pixels to their original values is sufficient to retain the
classification; and
\item[\textsc{exim}2.] $\vec V_{\mathrm{exp}}$ is minimal: no strict subset
satisfies \textsc{exim}1.
\end{itemize}

\noindent
This is the \emph{minimal sufficient pixel set} (\msps{}), grounded in
Halpern--Pearl actual causality rather than in gradients or surrogate models. An
\msps{} need not be intelligible to a human: it reports what the model accepts as
belonging to a class, which is often not what a person would point at.

Two caveats matter later. \rex{} approximates sufficient responsibility to build
its ranking, so \textsc{exim}2 is \emph{approximated}, not certified --- \rex{}
does not prove subset-minimality. And \textsc{exim}1 is a statement about
\emph{one} input, the one where the complement equals the masking value.

\subsection*{The family}

Kelly and Chockler split the single notion into four, which is useful because
``minimal and sufficient'' alone admits degenerate answers:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
\item \textbf{Sufficient} --- a set of components that justifies the
classifier's output: present them alone and the class is retained.
\item \textbf{Necessary} --- the minimal components \emph{required} for the
classification: remove them and the class changes. Sufficiency and necessity are
distinct, and an explanation can be one without being the other.
\item \textbf{$\delta$-complete} --- an explanation whose confidence meets a
threshold, rather than merely preserving the arg-max.
\item \textbf{$1$-complete} --- the limiting case: an explanation reaching the
same confidence as the model on the original image.
\end{itemize}

\noindent
The completeness notions are the practically important addition. A minimal
explanation can be minimal and still worthless --- the 20-pixel ladybird
``explanation'' in Section~4 carries $1.4\%$ confidence. In the tool this appears
as $\gamma$, a multiplicative factor on the model's confidence on the original
input, so that $\gamma = 0.5$ demands an explanation reaching half the original
confidence and $\gamma = 0$ demands nothing beyond the correct class. Every
number reported later is indexed by $\gamma$.

The authors' claim is that these give logic-comparable rigour while remaining
computable by a black-box algorithm: roughly 6\,s per ResNet image, with no
model internals, gradients, or monotonicity assumptions.

\section{Activation--Deactivation}

\subsection*{The problem it solves}

Perturbation-based explainability replaces the features it removes with a
\emph{masking value}, and the resulting mutants are frequently
out-of-distribution. The masking value is not neutral: if it carries meaning for
the model, the observed behaviour need not reflect behaviour on the real input.
Chanchal et al.\ show the choice materially changes the explanation, and that no
masking value is consistently best across models or datasets.

\ad{} removes the choice. Instead of giving masked features some value, it
\emph{switches off the parts of the model that would have read them}. The
information is not replaced; it is prevented from propagating.

\subsection*{The framework}

For layer $i$, the \emph{position function} $\mathrm{pos}_i(z^{(i)}_{ab})$ maps a
position in the intermediate representation back to the set of previous-layer
positions feeding it. The \emph{position-attribution function} measures how much
of that support survives a mask $M_i$:
\[
\Phi_i\!\left(z^{(i)}_{ab}, M_i\right)
= \frac{1}{\left|\mathrm{pos}_i(z^{(i)}_{ab})\right|}
\sum_{\mathrm{pos}_i(z^{(i)}_{ab})} \mathbf{1}\!\left(M_i[k,l] > 0\right)
\;\in [0,1].
\]
A threshold $\tau$ turns this into the next mask,
$M'_i[a,b] = \mathbf{1}(\Phi_i > \tau)$; at $\tau=0$ only fully masked regions
are deactivated. The \ad{} output at a \emph{checkpoint} after layer $N^{(i)}$ is
\[
O^{(i)} = N^{(i)}\!\left(z^{(i)}\right) \odot M'_i .
\]
Checkpoints are not every layer --- only those immediately before a resizing
operation, or before an operation that averages or converts data containing
deactivated values.

\subsection*{\convad{}}

The CNN instantiation propagates the mask by convolving it with a
\emph{mean-value kernel} (a matrix of ones normalised by its size) shaped like
the real kernel. The result gives the unmasked fraction at each position;
thresholding yields the next mask; activations whose entire receptive field was
masked are zeroed. Two checkpoint cases: shape-preserving between convolutions
(one checkpoint), and dimensionality-altering (two more, before and after ---
with the mask padded by $1$s or $0$s for additive procedures, or truncated for
subtractive ones).

Writing $N'$ for the instrumented model and $\mathbf{b}_1$ for the all-ones
mask, the key guarantee is
\[
\forall x,\quad N(x) = N'(x, \mathbf{b}_1). \tag{Thm.~4.1}
\]
On an empty mask, instrumented and original models are identical. This is the
formal difference from LayerMask (Balasubramanian and Feizi, ICCV 2023), which
offers no such guarantee and can disagree with the original model even when
nothing is masked.

\subsection*{Limitations the authors state}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
\item \textbf{Leakage.} $\tau$ is a threshold, so edge cases cannot be separated
cleanly: a convolution window straddling masked and unmasked regions; a window
covering both features and padding; and non-contiguous perturbations, where a
window overlaps several at once and no correct deactivation exists.
% \item \textbf{White-box.} The algorithm is identical across CNNs, but
% instrumenting a model needs its internals. \ad{} is model-agnostic \emph{within
% an architecture}, not black-box --- which costs it \rex{}'s main practical
% selling point.
% \item \textbf{CNN-only.} Transformers are future work.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Evaluation}

Three models (RegNetY-12GF, ResNet-50, EfficientNet-V2), three datasets
(ImageNet-1k, ImageNet-v2, PASCAL-VOC), 150 random validation images each;
explanations from \rex{}, \textsc{lime} and \textsc{grad-cam}, all reduced to a
common notion via the \rex{} extraction algorithm. Throughout $\tau=0$ --- the
strictest setting, which the authors note disadvantages their own method --- and
$\gamma \in \{0, 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9\}$.

Robustness $\beta$ is the proportion of a background set $K$ in which inserting
the explanation still yields the original class ($K_1$ solid colours, $K_2$
other-class images, 100 each). Headline results: \convad{} is $30$--$40\%$
better on average robustness and up to $22\%$ better on transferability, while
matching the best methods on redundancy. There is \emph{no consistent best
masking value} across models or datasets. Overlap analysis shows masking
strategies resemble each other (\textsc{dice} $0.56$--$0.78$) but not \convad{}
(max $0.2$--$0.47$), so \convad{} finds different explanations rather than
supersets.

\section{\rex{} explanations vs.\ abductive explanations}

\subsection*{The two definitions}

Recall \textsc{exim}1: the complement is filled with \emph{one} masking value
$m$, and the test is
\[
N(x_A \oplus m_B) = N(x),
\qquad B = \Theta(x) \setminus A .
\]
\verix{} instead quantifies universally over an $\varepsilon$-ball:
\[
\forall x_B'.\;\; \left\lVert x_B - x_B' \right\rVert_p \le \varepsilon
\;\Rightarrow\; \left| f(x) - f(x') \right| \le \delta,
\tag{$*$}
\]
and calls the explanation \emph{optimal} when in addition every retained feature
admits a counterexample --- i.e.\ none is redundant.

In the vocabulary of space explanations, a formula $\varphi$ with
$\forall x.\, \varphi(x) \Rightarrow \kappa(x) = c$ has impact space
$\mathcal{F}_\varphi = \{x \mid \varphi(x)\}$. An abductive explanation
$\varphi_X := \bigwedge_{i \in X} x_i = s_i$ leaves the complement free over the
whole domain, so its impact space is a line, prism or hyperrectangle of positive
volume. By contrast:

\begin{quote}
\textbf{The impact space of a \rex{} \msps{} is a single point} --- the one
completion where $B = m$. It entails nothing about any other point of
$\mathcal{F}$.
\end{quote}

So a \rex{} explanation is \emph{not} a space explanation, and minimality does
not repair this. The two are not stronger and weaker versions of one thing; they
quantify differently.

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
& \textbf{\rex{} \msps{}} & \textbf{Abductive / \verix{}} \\
\midrule
Complement $B$   & one masking value $m$      & universally quantified \\
Impact space     & a single point             & positive-volume region \\
Guarantee        & empirical                  & provable (sound+complete oracle) \\
Minimality       & approximated               & optimal, by construction \\
Model access     & black-box                  & white-box (SMT / \textsc{Marabou}) \\
Cost             & seconds, $\sim\!10^4$ passes & $O(d \cdot P(f))$, $P(f)$ NP-complete \\
Tolerance        & $\gamma$ (relative conf.)  & $\delta$ (absolute) \\
Traversal        & responsibility map         & sensitivity with $T(\chi)=0$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection*{Two bridges}

\textbf{Robustness estimates the universal quantifier.} Measuring $\beta$ by
pasting an \msps{} onto $K$ backgrounds is a Monte-Carlo approximation of
exactly the $\forall$ in $(*)$, with $B$ ranging over sampled completions rather
than an $\varepsilon$-ball. An explanation with $\beta = 1$ over \emph{all}
completions would be an abductive explanation. So $\beta$ reads as distance from
abductive validity --- which is how the numbers in the next section should be
interpreted.

\textbf{Three answers to one question.} What do you do with the features left
out?
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
\item \rex{}, \textsc{lime}, \textsc{shap}: substitute a value --- a \emph{point estimate}.
\item Abductive / \verix{} / space explanations: universally quantify --- a \emph{region}.
\item \ad{}: assign nothing; \emph{delete the computation} that would have read them.
\end{itemize}
The third is genuinely distinct, and is what makes \ad{} interesting from a
formal-methods standpoint rather than just another saliency method. The o.o.d.\
critique motivating \ad{} is precisely the observation that a point estimate is
an arbitrary, semantically loaded choice --- the pathology universal
quantification exists to exclude.

\section{What I measured}

\rex{} v0.4.1 on ResNet-50 and RegNetY-12GF with the authors' instrumented
models, five ImageNet images ($224\times224 = 50{,}176$ px). Five defects in the
released tool needed repair before any non-$224\times224$ image would process.

\textbf{Thm.~4.1 holds exactly.} $N(x)$ against $N'(x,\mathbf{b}_1)$ over the
logit vector: max absolute difference $0.000$.

\textbf{The o.o.d.\ problem is real.} Keeping only a central $80\times80$ patch
of the ladybird image:

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lc@{}}
\toprule
Condition & Confidence in original class \\
\midrule
Full image                       & $0.4138$ \\
Complement occluded with black   & $\mathbf{0.4276}$ \\
Complement deactivated (\convad{}) & $0.0987$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Occluding to black \emph{raised} confidence above the unmasked image: absence of
evidence was read as evidence for the class. This is how \rex{} produces a
20-pixel ``explanation'' at $\gamma=0$, and a concrete instance of what $(*)$
exists to exclude.

\textbf{Sizes} (mean over 5 images, px out of 50{,}176, ResNet-50):

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$\gamma$ & zero & min & mean & \convad{} \\
\midrule
$0.0$ & $\mathbf{1284}$ & $3018$ & $1666$ & $1794$ \\
$0.5$ & $\mathbf{2258}$ & $4176$ & $2898$ & $4126$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
\convad{} does \emph{not} give the smallest explanations, and that is the point:
black occlusion wins on size precisely by exploiting the artefact above. Size
alone is a misleading quality metric.

\textbf{Robustness, re-measured.} \rex{}'s built-in analysis is degenerate for
\ad{} --- it calls the script's own prediction function, which deactivates
everything outside the mask, so the background it claims to test against is
invisible to the model. It returned exactly $1.0$ in all twelve \convad{} runs
against a mean of $0.10$ for masking. That figure is an artefact and should not
be quoted. Re-scoring every \msps{} on the \emph{unmodified} ResNet-50 with a
common protocol (17 backgrounds: 8 solid, 5 dataset images, 4 noise fields):

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
Method & Mean size (px) & Retention $\beta$ \\
\midrule
plain, zero & $2258$ & $0.329$ \\
plain, mean & $2898$ & $0.376$ \\
plain, min  & $4176$ & $0.506$ \\
\convad{}   & $4126$ & $\mathbf{0.647}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Size-controlled: \convad{} vs \texttt{min} at essentially equal size ($4126$ vs
$4176$) gives $0.647$ vs $0.506$, a $28\%$ relative gain --- consistent with the
paper's $30$--$40\%$. Read through the bridge above: \emph{no} \rex{}
explanation under any strategy approaches $\beta=1$, so none is an abductive
explanation, though \convad{}'s are measurably nearer. Qualitatively,
occlusion-based \msps{} often select background artefacts (flowers beside a
peacock, rock beside a starfish) where \convad{} selects the object.

\emph{Caveat:} 5 images, one architecture. Enough to corroborate direction, not
to independently validate the paper's 150-image claims.

\section{Does \ad{} transfer to fully-connected networks?}

\subsection*{Why the published algorithm is vacuous}

\ad{}'s content is the position function: the receptive field of an output cell.
In a fully-connected layer every neuron reads every input, so
$\mathrm{pos}_i(z^{(i)}_a)$ is the whole previous layer and
\[
\Phi_i\!\left(z^{(i)}_a, M_i\right)
= \frac{\left|\{k : M_i[k] > 0\}\right|}{\left|M_i\right|}
\]
is \emph{the same scalar for every neuron} --- the global unmasked fraction. At
$\tau=0$ no neuron is deactivated until the entire input is masked, at which
point all are. \ad{} as published is a no-op on an MLP.

There is a second point. At the \emph{first} layer \ad{} and zero-masking
already coincide: $Wx = W_A x_A + W_B x_B$, so with zero bias, deleting $B$'s
contribution \emph{is} setting $x_B = 0$ (the authors' ResNet code says exactly
this). All of \ad{}'s value therefore lies in layers $\ge 2$, and consists in
protecting spatial structure across depth. An MLP has none: past layer one every
neuron mixes everything.

\subsection*{Three analogues}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em,label=(\roman*)]
\item \textbf{Weight-mass attribution.} Replace the mean-value kernel with
actual weight magnitudes,
$\Phi(z_a, M) = \sum_{i \notin M} |W_{ai}| \big/ \sum_i |W_{ai}|$, deactivating
when $\Phi < \tau$. This restores a meaningful ``this neuron is mostly looking
at things that aren't there''. Note $\tau=0$ is the degenerate case here, so
unlike in \convad{} the threshold becomes mandatory --- an unexplored direction
the paper itself flags.

\item \textbf{Neuron-level ablation.} Compute per-neuron relevance mass from the
masked features (one $|W|$ propagation pass, or an LRP-style attribution) and
zero the units dominated by them. ``Ablate the sub-network that reads $B$'' ---
closer in spirit to \ad{} than (i).

\item \textbf{Fixed activation pattern, column deletion.} For a ReLU net, fix
the active-ReLU set induced by the \emph{original} input. On that region the
network is affine, $f(x) = W_{\mathrm{eff}} x + b_{\mathrm{eff}}$, and
deactivating $B$ is exactly
$W_{\mathrm{eff},A}\, x_A + b_{\mathrm{eff}}$ --- deleting columns of an
explicit matrix.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent
\textbf{(iii) is the one to pursue.} It alone both preserves \ad{}'s defining
property (removal, not substitution) and carries a guarantee: the Thm.~4.1
analogue is trivial, since the all-ones mask deletes no columns. It is also
\emph{linear}, hence free in an SMT encoding --- no ITE terms, no case splits ---
which is what makes the next section possible. Its cost: the affine restriction
holds only inside one activation region, so the guarantee is local by
construction. That is a real limitation, but no worse than the linear
relaxations already standard in NN verification.

\section{Using \ad{} in an SMT pipeline}

The pipeline encodes the network as $\psi_{\mathcal{M}}$, domains as
$\psi_{\mathcal{D}}$, the negated class as $\neg\psi_c$, so
$\psi \equiv \psi_{\mathcal{M}} \wedge \psi_{\mathcal{D}} \wedge \neg\psi_c$ is
satisfiable while $\varphi_s \wedge \psi$ is unsatisfiable for
$\varphi_s \equiv x=s$; explanation weakens $\varphi_s$ while preserving
unsatisfiability, via Craig interpolation or unsat cores. The two complaints ---
slow, and formulas too complicated --- are different problems, and \ad{}
addresses them by different mechanisms.

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}clll@{}}
\toprule
& \textbf{Use of \ad{}} & \textbf{Targets} & \textbf{Soundness} \\
\midrule
1 & traversal / partition heuristic & speed + size   & unaffected \\
2 & seed for guess-and-check        & speed          & unaffected \\
3 & restrict interpolant support    & readability    & unaffected \\
4 & prune the encoding itself       & speed (large)  & \textbf{proves $N'$, not $N$} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection*{1. Traversal heuristic}

\verix{} ranks features by sensitivity under $T(\chi)=0$ --- deletion, i.e.\
occlusion, i.e.\ exactly what \ad{} discredits. Substitute an \ad{}-based
ranking,
$\mathrm{sens}_{\ad{}}(\chi_i) = f(x) - f_{\ad{}}(x, M = \Theta\setminus i)$, or
simply the \rex{} responsibility map computed under \convad{}, which the tool
already exposes as a ranked list. Better ordering means genuinely irrelevant
features are tested first, absorbed early, and never reach the explanation ---
giving both a smaller $A$ and fewer solver calls returning ``must keep''.
Soundness is untouched: order is only a heuristic, the guarantee comes from the
oracle. In the interpolation setting the analogue is the choice of
$\varphi_A$ vs $\varphi_B$.

\subsection*{2. \ad{}-seeded guess-and-check}

Run \rex{}+\convad{} black-box (seconds) for a candidate $X_0$, then ask one
question: is $\varphi_{X_0} \wedge \psi$ unsatisfiable?

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
\item \textsc{unsat} --- $X_0$ is a verified abductive explanation, in \emph{one}
query instead of $d$.
\item \textsc{sat} --- the counterexample names a feature being exploited; add it
and repeat.
\end{itemize}

\noindent
Minimality, if needed, is recovered afterwards by a shrinking loop. The reason to
seed with \ad{} rather than occlusion is quantitative: a zero-masking \msps{} is
fitted to one point of $B$'s domain and will usually fail a universally
quantified check, and each failure is a wasted expensive query. The retention
figures ($0.647$ vs $0.329$) estimate first-shot success rate directly ---
roughly twice as many \ad{} candidates should survive, hence roughly half the
refinement rounds.

\subsection*{3. Restricting interpolant support}

The readability complaint is that interpolants are conjunctions of arbitrary
linear combinations $\sum_i \alpha_i x_i \le \beta$ over all $d$ features. The
survey already notes that if a space explanation splits as
$\varphi_A \wedge \varphi_B$, then for $I = \mathit{Itp}(\varphi_A, \varphi_B
\wedge \psi)$ the formula $I \wedge \varphi_B$ is a weaker space explanation ---
so interpolation can be focused on features appearing only in $\varphi_A$.
\ad{} supplies a principled choice of that subset, cutting the interpolant from
$O(10^4$--$10^5)$ to $O(10^3)$ features. The qualitative evidence matters here:
occlusion-based saliency would hand the interpolator scattered background
artefacts; \convad{} gives coherent regions of the object.

\subsection*{4. Pruning the encoding}

Under a committed mask, \convad{} makes an identifiable set of neurons
identically zero. Those can be dropped from $\psi_{\mathcal{M}}$ outright: no
ITE term, no ReLU case split. Since ReLU splits are the source of exponential
blow-up, this is potentially the largest saving of the four.

The caveat is essential: Thm.~4.1 guarantees $N = N'$ \emph{only} on the
all-ones mask; under any real mask they differ by construction. Pruning
therefore proves a property of $N'$, not $N$. Two ways to stay sound: use $N'$
for \emph{search} only and confirm the final candidate on $N$ (i.e.\ the loop
above); or state the result about $N'$ deliberately --- defensible on the \ad{}
paper's own terms, since it argues $N'$ is the more faithful object, but a
formal-methods audience will reasonably object.

\subsection*{Obstacles}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
\item \textbf{\convad{} is CNN-only.} If the target networks are the
fully-connected models typical of this literature, construction~(iii) above is a
prerequisite, not plumbing.
\item \textbf{Scale.} SMT explanations run at $d \approx 784$; \rex{}/\ad{} at
$50{,}176$. The win grows with $d$, so on small networks the \rex{} run may cost
more than the solver calls it saves. Worth measuring before committing.
\item \textbf{Granularity.} \rex{} reasons over boxes, SMT over individual
features. Not purely an obstacle --- the box hierarchy suits coarse-to-fine
verification.
\end{itemize}

\section{Bottom line}

\ad{} identifies a real and under-appreciated confound and removes it by
construction rather than by a better heuristic. Thm.~4.1 gives it a footing
LayerMask lacks, and the gains survive an independent, size-controlled
re-evaluation. The mean-value-kernel trick is simple enough to port to a new
architecture in a day.

Against that: $\tau=0$ everywhere, so the framework's only hyperparameter is
entirely unexplored; leakage is acknowledged but never quantified; the
evaluation rests on proxy metrics, as the authors concede; and white-box
instrumentation costs \rex{} its black-box selling point. The reported
robustness also depends on \emph{which} prediction function computes it --- a
subtlety that, as the measurements above show, can invert the conclusion if
handled carelessly.

For the SMT work the useful framing is this. \ad{} does not produce abductive
explanations and does not claim to; its contribution is a better notion of
\emph{feature removal}, which any perturbation method can adopt. It makes the
point-estimate answer strictly better without making it a region answer --- and
the gap between $\beta = 0.647$ and $\beta = 1$ is exactly the gap that
universal quantification closes.

\section*{The papers}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
\item \textbf{\rex{}} --- Chockler, Kelly, Kroening, Sun, \emph{Causal Explanations
for Image Classifiers}, JAIR 86 (2026), arXiv:2411.08875. Defines the notion of
explanation and gives a black-box algorithm computing it.
\item \textbf{Taxonomy} --- Kelly, Chockler, \emph{Sufficient, Necessary and
Complete Causal Explanations}, arXiv:2507.23497. Splits that notion into a
family; see Section~1.
\item \textbf{\ad{}} --- Chanchal, Kelly, Chockler, \emph{Activation--Deactivation},
arXiv:2510.01038v2. The subject of most of what follows.
\end{itemize}

\noindent
Also referenced: \verix{} (Wu, Wu, Barrett, arXiv:2212.01051); the FM~2026 survey
\emph{Formally Explaining Neural Network Classification}; and \emph{Space
Explanations}, arXiv:2511.22498.

